# AgentFlow working contract

Use one method: domain-first MVI with typed commands.

1. Domain owns framework-independent values, rules, IDs, revisions and errors.
2. A Feature owns value State, Action and Operation. Its reducer is synchronous
   and pure: `(inout State, Action) -> [Command<Operation>]`.
3. The MainActor Store alone owns mutable feature state and command task lifetime.
4. UI renders state and sends actions. Local focus or animation state may stay
   in the view; do not copy domain or workflow state into a ViewModel.
5. Command describes work. The app's runner uses explicit Sendable ports to
   perform it and returns result actions. Keep I/O out of reducers and Domain.
6. Compose child values and mapped commands into the same state owner. A read
   projection or send facade must not create another Store or task registry.
7. Runtime tokens, cancellation keys, request IDs and durable operation IDs have
   different jobs. Preserve each at its actual authority boundary.
8. Revoke stale delivery before cooperative cancellation. Completion must not
   remove a replacement task's registration. Never treat cancellation as rollback.
9. A database or provider adapter owns transactions, CAS, durable receipts and
   unknown-outcome recovery. Do not put a generic business retry policy here.
10. Do not drop actions or receipts to coalesce UI snapshots. Do not retry a
    write whose outcome is unknown without its adapter's reconciliation contract.
11. Use one pinned remote package in each dependency graph. Keep app state,
    data formats and routing outside this SDK.
12. Support Swift 6 language mode with the documented tools/deployment floors.
    Do not add newer APIs because a development machine happens to support them.
13. Change the smallest complete owner and its required consumers. Add an
    abstraction only when actual shared contracts justify it.
14. Report source changes, executed checks, and unverified boundaries separately.
    Do not claim that source review or package tests prove app/runtime readiness.
